			ctx = templ.InitializeContext(ctx)
			templ_7745c5c3_Err = templruntime.WriteString(templ_7745c5c3_Buffer, 16, "<h1>How it works</h1><p>In Cook County, your property tax bill depends on your home's assessed value. The Assessor sets it with a model that values every home in the county, and the Board of Review hears appeals from owners who think theirs is too high.</p><h2>What we compare</h2><p>One ground the Board of Review accepts is <em>lack of uniformity</em>: similar homes nearby are assessed lower than yours. We look up homes in your Assessor neighborhood with the same property class (or the same broad group), similar size (within 25% of your square footage) and similar age (within 15 years). Then we rank them by how closely they match your home's size, age, bathrooms, lot, style, exterior and basement.</p><p>For each one, we divide assessed value by building square footage. If yours is well above the median of similar homes, that's the kind of evidence a uniformity appeal uses.</p><h2>How we describe the comparison</h2><p>We don't tell you whether to appeal. We show how your assessed value per square foot compares with the median of similar homes, how many of them are assessed lower than yours, and how appeals have gone for homeowners in your neighborhood. We offer the free evidence report (a PDF) when at least 3 similar homes are assessed lower than yours and your home is at least 1% above the median.</p><h2>If an appeal doesn't work</h2><p>Each tax year you can appeal to the Assessor and then to the Board of Review. If the Board of Review doesn't lower your assessment, you can appeal to the state Property Tax Appeal Board. And you can appeal again the next year.</p><p>We tested this against about 400,000 Board of Review decisions from 2024 and 2025. Among homeowners who appealed on their own, homes assessed at least 5% above similar homes got a reduction about 82% of the time. Homes 0–5% above got one about 72% of the time, and homes below the median 27–53% of the time. People who appeal usually already have a reason to, so these rates aren't a prediction for any one home.</p><h2>What we don't do</h2><ul><li>We don't estimate what your home would sell for, and nothing here is an appraisal.</li><li>We aren't a law firm and don't give legal advice. We don't file anything for you or represent you.</li><li>We aren't affiliated with the Assessor, the Board of Review or any government office.</li></ul><h2>Where the data comes from</h2><p>Everything comes from Cook County's public open data portal: parcel characteristics, assessed values, sales and Board of Review appeal outcomes. We update our copy as the county publishes new data. If the county's data about your home is wrong, our comparison will be too, so check the details we show.</p>")
